  you'd rather I design only the notification layer against an assumed task shape.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what "tasks they care about" resolves to. This becomes stored data and shows up in the server version
  unchanged, so it's the expensive one to get wrong.

  - [1mExplicit subscribe (star/follow a task):[0m user opts in per task. Clear intent, no noise, trivially explainable. Costs
    a UI affordance and means users get nothing until they remember to subscribe — cold-start silence.
  - [1mImplicit by relationship (creator/assignee):[0m you're notified about tasks that are yours. Zero effort, useful
    immediately. Requires a notion of task ownership, and you can't follow something that isn't yours without adding
    explicit subscribe later anyway.
  - [1mBoth, with implicit as the default:[0m ownership auto-subscribes, plus a manual follow toggle and an unfollow. Covers
    both cold-start and interest in others' tasks. Slightly more state per task; it's the union, so nothing is ruled
    out.
  - [1mRules/filters (notify on tag, priority, etc.):[0m most flexible, and the one I'd push back on — it's a query engine
    with a notification system attached, and YAGNI applies hard at this stage.

  [1mMy recommendation: both, implicit default.[0m It's barely more data than the implicit-only version (a subscriber set per
  task rather than a single owner field), and it's the shape a server version would want regardless — so choosing it now
  avoids a migration rather than buying speculative flexibility.
